WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:05.600
From the opening credits, Men in Black: International
feels every bit like the rest of the franchise

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.980
- just with better CGI and some fresh faces.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:12.020
It also makes clever nods to its past, as
well as other familiar movies.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.640
Here are a few small details you may have
missed!

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.420
It's become difficult to separate Chris Hemsworth
from the thunder god Thor - the Asgardian

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:21.500
Avenger of Marvel's superhero movies.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:26.380
With Hemsworth starring as the hammer-swinging
hero in three solo films and all four Avengers

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:30.750
installments, it's safe to say there are plenty
of moviegoers who might not know who Hemsworth

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.860
was if it weren't for his MCU flicks.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:37.600
It can be a problem when an actor so closely
associated with one role stars in something

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:42.400
different, but rather than ignore the proverbial
elephant in the room, Men in Black: International

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.960
has some clever fun with it.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.460
Towards the end of the film, Agents H and
M, and their sidekick Pawny, infiltrate the

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.500
fortress of an alien arms dealer.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.560
Agent H gets into a fight with the dealer's
super strong bodyguard and he's clearly outmatched.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:01.399
At one point, H spots a small hammer and grabs
it, but H isn't nearly as skilled in hammer-based

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.880
combat as Thor...

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.520
"Looks like the tables have turned!

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:08.760
That was an incredible catch."

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.300
Agent H's brief hammer-related delusion of
grandeur isn't the only nod to the MCU.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:19.420
In a battle against twin alien villains, Agent
H bravely steps forward and warns the villains

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:21.080
that they're facing the Men in Black.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.820
Then, he corrects himself...

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.560
"The Men and Women in Black."

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.880
"Yeah, perfectly done."

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.480
The moment works even without the reference,
but it's nonetheless a nod to a scene Hemsworth

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.780
and Tessa Thompson share in 2017's Thor: Ragnarok.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:41.140
While Thor is still a slave and is about to
be sent into the arena to fight, Thor sees

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:46.240
a tattoo on Thompson's yet-to-be-named character's
arm that marks her as one of the Valkyrie,

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.820
an elite force of Asgardian warrior women.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:53.500
Desperately trying to get in her good graces
so she'll help him escape, Thor stumbles over

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.360
his words before flashing Thompson a similar
smile and a thumbs up...

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:02.080
"I think it's great that there is an elite
force of women warriors.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.360
It's about time."

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:11.540
In 1997's Men in Black, shortly after Agent
J is accepted into MiB, Agent K brings the

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:13.180
new recruit to the armory.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:18.780
K chooses an impressive Series 4 De-Atomizer
for himself while handing J a teeny, tiny

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.440
thing that looks like it might be a keychain
or a fancy kitchen gadget.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.160
He calls it the "Noisy Cricket" and J is unimpressed.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.360
Of course, J later learns the Noisy Cricket
is one of many things in Men in Black that

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.960
should not be judged by its outer appearance.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:43.540
Apparently the Noisy Cricket is still in use,
and MiB isn't any better about educating new

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:44.480
agents about it.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.700
Shortly after Agent M is accepted as a probationary
agent, we get a montage similar to the one

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.560
we see in 1997's Men in Black when Agent J
is inducted.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:59.060
M is given a new wardrobe, sunglasses to protect
her from the amnesia-inducing Neuralizers,

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:00.860
and then it's time to choose her weapon.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:05.940
Agent M lifts the Noisy Cricket daintily out
of its case and, looking at the camera, lets

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.480
us know there's no way she'll be trusting
this dinky little thing with her safety.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:14.080
Unfortunately, there's no Agent K around to
let her know just how much stuff that little

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:15.080
thing can blow up.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.600
If you're worried Men in Black: International
is yet another Hollywood reboot, some small

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.000
details let you know that isn't the case.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.700
There's Agent O, of course, who first appeared
in Men in Black 3.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:30.319
But there are also some artistic details in
the office of Agent High T, the head of the

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.060
agency's London branch.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:37.080
Men in Black: International opens on Agents
H and High T, a few years earlier, teaming

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.400
up to fight aliens known as the Hive.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.200
The villains are coming to Earth via a portal
in Paris' Eiffel Tower.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.680
The prologue cuts away before we see how that
scenario ends, but we later hear different

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.880
agents speaking of it with great reverence.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:54.220
We also see a painting meant to represent
this victory in Agent High T's office.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:57.740
But this Paris battle isn't the only immortalized
moment.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:02.040
Blink and you'll miss it, but not far from
the Paris painting is one of the more familiar

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:07.200
J and K in the final battle of 1997's Men
in Black as they battle the giant bug at the

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.580
observation towers of the New York World's
Fair.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:13.940
So while we never learn what happened to J
and K, the painting lets us know International

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:17.200
is a continuation of the series rather than
a do-over.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:21.720
Men in Black: International does a good job
of paying tribute to the earlier films without

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.720
getting too bogged down in the past.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:28.020
Perfect examples include a pair of cameos
from popular aliens we've seen before.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:32.680
When Molly first bluffs her way into the MiB
headquarters, the old man watching the elevator

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:35.800
is accompanied by a pug in a dog bed at his
feet.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:41.560
Once Molly slips into the elevator, the pug
says "Talk about amateur hour," and we instantly

00:04:41.569 --> 00:04:45.490
recognize the voice of Tim Blaney, the actor
who did the voice of Frank the Pug in the

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:47.100
first two Men in Black films.

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:48.000
"I know nothing."

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.620
"Not a thing, huh?"

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:55.719
Later, after she's discovered and accepted
into the agency, Agent M walks past a group

00:04:55.719 --> 00:05:01.189
of short, antennaed, wisecracking aliens instantly
recognizable as the same guys that shocked

00:05:01.189 --> 00:05:05.020
Agent J when he saw them hanging out in the
MiB break room in the first film.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:10.319
When we meet Agent H, he's involved in an
undercover op, posing as a gambler for some

00:05:10.319 --> 00:05:15.099
kind of alien card game where players have
to reach into a cage holding a poisonous three-headed

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.000
snake in order to get their cards.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:22.860
The game is overseen by an alien crook named
Luca Brasi, a pretty blatant reference to

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.260
1972's classic mafia epic The Godfather.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:29.599
In The Godfather, Luca Brasi is strangled
to death while trying to infiltrate a rival

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:30.340
gang.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:34.100
The character was played Lenny Montana, a
former professional wrestler as well as a

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.960
real-life mob enforcer.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:40.680
The Corleones learn of Brasi's murder when
a package is delivered including fish wrapped

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:44.800
in Brasi's vest, leading to one of the film's
most famous lines.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.180
If you caught the reference in Men in Black:
International, you probably guessed the fate

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:50.960
of the alien Luca Brasi...

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.720
"Luca Brasi sleeps with the fishes."

